    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A church built around 1200, still standing, marks Järfälla’s early Christian heritage after the town was Christianized in the 11th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%A4rf%C3%A4lla_Municipality"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Medieval roads that crossed Järfälla made the municipality a key stop in regional trade during the Middle Ages.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%A4rf%C3%A4lla_Municipality"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The municipality borders Lake Mälaren, Sweden’s third‑largest lake, giving Järfälla a shoreline that shapes local recreation and hydrology.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/J%C3%A4rf%C3%A4lla_Municipality"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Järfälla experiences a humid continental climate, with cold, snowy winters and cool to mild summers influenced by its inland position near Stockholm.",
